.TH at "" "Command"
.SH NAME
at \- execute commands at given time
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBat [\-v] [\-c \fIcommand\fB] \fItime \fB[[\fIday\fB] \fIweek\fB] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.br
\fBat [\-v] [\-c \fIcommand\fB] \fItime month day \fB[\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
at -- execute commands at given time
Usage:	at [ -v ] [ -c command ] time [ [ day ] week ] [ file ]
        at [ -v ] [ -c command ] time month day [ file ]
Options:
	-c	Following argument gives command
	-v	Print time command will be executed
If 'file' is given, take commands from it.
If neither file nor '-c' is given, read commands from standard input.
.HE
.B at
takes a list of commands and arranges for them to be executed at the
given
.IR time .
When the commands run, the user id, group id, exported shell
variables, and current directory are all as they were when
.B at
was invoked.
.PP
.I time
is one to four digits giving hours and optionally minutes, followed
optionally by
.BR a ,
.BR p ,
.BR n ,
or
.B m
for A.M., P.M., noon, or midnight.
.I time
may be followed by a
.I day
of the week, optionally followed by the word
.B week
to mean the following week, or by a
.I month
name and a numeric day of the month.
If no day or month is given, the command runs at the next occurrence
of
.I time
today or tomorrow.
.PP
.B at
recognizes the following options:
.IP \fB\-c\ \fIcommand\fR
Take the commands to run from the string
.I command
rather than from standard input or
.IR file .
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Print the date and time at which the command will be executed.
.PP
If a
.I file
argument is given,
.B at
takes the commands to run from it.
If neither
.I file
nor
.B \-c
is given,
.B at
reads the commands from the standard input.
.PP
.B at
copies the commands, the current environment, umask, and working
directory into a shell script in directory
.BR /usr/spool/at ,
named for the year, month, day, hour, and minute at which it is to
run, and sets the set-user-id, set-group-id, and owner-execute bits
on that file.
The command
.B atrun
watches this directory and, when the time arrives, runs the script
with the user and group id recorded in its permission bits.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.5i
	\fB/usr/spool/at\fR	spool directory for pending at jobs
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR atrun ,
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B at
complains and exits with a nonzero status if it cannot parse
.IR time ,
cannot create its spool file, or cannot find the command
.B pwd
needed to record the current working directory.
.SH NOTES
.B at
must not be installed set-user-id or set-group-id: anyone may write
into the
.B at
spooling directory, and security instead rests on
.B at
itself setting the set-user-id, set-group-id, and owner-execute bits
on the file it creates; without all three,
.B atrun
will refuse to run the script.
